WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.940
At rest, each side of an adult human heart pumps about five litres of blood a

2
00:00:07.940 --> 00:00:12.220
minute. Over any few minutes, it cannot pump out more than flows back in.

3
00:00:12.640 --> 00:00:14.340
So what sets cardiac output?

4
00:00:14.860 --> 00:00:16.700
The heart, or the veins that fill it?

5
00:00:17.633 --> 00:00:20.093
The Fick principle is bookkeeping for oxygen.

6
00:00:20.693 --> 00:00:24.473
At steady state, the oxygen the lungs take up each minute leaves in the blood

7
00:00:24.473 --> 00:00:25.473
flowing through them.

8
00:00:25.793 --> 00:00:27.573
Take made-up numbers for an adult at rest.

9
00:00:28.173 --> 00:00:31.133
Uptake is 260 mL O2 per min.

10
00:00:31.753 --> 00:00:37.233
Arterial blood holds 195 mL per L. Mixed-venous blood from the pulmonary artery

11
00:00:37.233 --> 00:00:40.573
holds 143. The difference is 52.

12
00:00:40.573 --> 00:00:44.693
260 divided by 52 is 5 L per min.

13
00:00:45.683 --> 00:00:48.843
Stop the heart, and the whole circulation settles at one pressure.

14
00:00:49.383 --> 00:00:52.883
That zero-flow pressure is close to the mean systemic filling pressure.

15
00:00:53.483 --> 00:00:57.583
In a tank, fluid below the outlet rim, the unstressed volume, fills it without

16
00:00:57.583 --> 00:00:58.583
stretching the walls.

17
00:00:59.383 --> 00:01:02.863
Fluid above the rim, the stressed volume, stretches them and pushes back.

18
00:01:03.643 --> 00:01:04.943
Adding blood raises the pressure.

19
00:01:05.763 --> 00:01:11.823
Guyton's group measured about 6 to 7 mmHg in anaesthetized dogs, a dog value, not a

20
00:01:11.823 --> 00:01:12.823
normal human one.

21
00:01:13.667 --> 00:01:17.887
Guyton's group replaced the right heart with an external pump, in open-chest dogs.

22
00:01:18.427 --> 00:01:20.967
They set right atrial pressure and measured the return.

23
00:01:21.547 --> 00:01:25.067
As they lowered the pressure, return rose along a roughly straight line.

24
00:01:25.547 --> 00:01:30.387
It stopped rising at about minus -2 to minus -4 mmHg, because veins collapse.

25
00:01:31.167 --> 00:01:34.307
Return reached zero where atrial pressure equals filling pressure.

26
00:01:34.787 --> 00:01:36.927
That line is the venous return curve.

27
00:01:36.927 --> 00:01:42.087
Flow equals filling pressure minus atrial pressure, divided by the resistance to

28
00:01:42.087 --> 00:01:43.087
venous return.

29
00:01:43.850 --> 00:01:45.270
The heart has a curve too.

30
00:01:45.850 --> 00:01:50.530
At fixed chest pressure, higher atrial pressure means more filling, so output rises.

31
00:01:51.450 --> 00:01:55.830
Averaged over time, output equals venous return, so the circulation settles where

32
00:01:55.830 --> 00:01:59.870
the lines cross. This is an illustrative model, not fitted to data.

33
00:02:00.530 --> 00:02:03.870
3 plus Pra equals 7 minus Pra.

34
00:02:04.350 --> 00:02:08.930
That gives atrial pressure 2 mmHg, output 5 L per min.

35
00:02:09.933 --> 00:02:11.993
In the model, move one line at a time.

36
00:02:12.473 --> 00:02:14.013
A stronger heart lifts its line.

37
00:02:14.573 --> 00:02:17.433
Output rises to 6, and atrial pressure falls to 1.

38
00:02:17.753 --> 00:02:18.993
A weaker heart drops it.

39
00:02:19.413 --> 00:02:22.353
Output falls to 4, and atrial pressure rises to 3.

40
00:02:23.033 --> 00:02:24.533
Venoconstriction acts in seconds.

41
00:02:24.913 --> 00:02:29.273
Over hours to days, retained salt and water raise filling pressure to 9, and the

42
00:02:29.273 --> 00:02:30.553
veins' line slides right.

43
00:02:31.133 --> 00:02:33.853
Output is back to 5, atrial pressure at 4.

44
00:02:34.433 --> 00:02:38.473
The rule to keep: output sits where the heart's line crosses the veins' line.
